      "problem": "$-21\\text{ mod }5=$",
      "worked_solution": "The residue of $-21$ modulo $5$ is the integer $0\\le r<5$ such that\n\n$$\n-21≡r\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $-21$ as an integer multiple of $5,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $-21≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$ because\n\n$$\n-21=(-5)\\cdot 5+4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-21$ modulo $5$ is $4.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the addition property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-21 & ≡-21+0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡-21+5\\cdot 5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡-21+25 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-21$ modulo $5$ is $4.$",
